<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Sample Past Exam 2, with solutions.

  Transcribed from the instructor's solutions PDF of a past second midterm
  (MATH13-Exam2-Solutions): the seven exam questions and their solutions,
  with the figures of Questions 3 and 4 redrawn in TikZ.  As requested,
  the point values were left out, and the introduction states the exam's
  rules in the same words as Sample Past Exam 1.  That exam had no formula
  sheet, so none is reproduced here.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="worksheet-sample-past-exam-2" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Sample Past Exam 2</title>

<introduction>
<p>
A sample past exam, with worked solutions. It was a 65-minute, closed-book
exam: no aids of their own (formula sheets, notes, phones, tablets, smart
watches or similar) were permitted, and students were asked to write their
work in a neat and organized format and to fully justify every step. Try
the exam under the same conditions before you look at the solutions. The
topics it covers, with links to the notes:
</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li><p><xref ref="chapter-vectors" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-dot-product" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-cross-product" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-parametrization" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="chapter-lines-planes" text="title"/></p></li>
<li><p><xref ref="section-conics" text="title"/></p></li>
</ul>
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ===================== Question 1 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe2-q1">

<task xml:id="spe2-q1-a">
<statement>
<p>
Compute the vector <m>\vec v</m> whose tail and tip are at
<m>(2,0,-1)</m> and <m>\left(3,2,-1+\sqrt{11}\right)</m>, respectively.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
\vec v = \left\langle 3-2,\ 2-0,\ -1+\sqrt{11}-(-1)\right\rangle
= \left\langle 1,\ 2,\ \sqrt{11}\right\rangle,
</md>
and
<md>
\|\vec v\| = \sqrt{1+4+11} = \sqrt{16} = 4.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe2-q1-b">
<statement>
<p>
Find a vector <m>\vec w</m> that points in the opposite direction to
<m>\vec v</m> (see part (a)) and whose length is triple the length of
<m>\vec v</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Opposite direction and three times as long:
<md>
\vec w = -3\vec v = \left\langle -3,\ -6,\ -3\sqrt{11}\right\rangle
</md>
(check: <m>\|\vec w\| = 3\cdot 4 = 12</m>).
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe2-q1-c">
<statement>
<p>
Find the unit vector in the direction of <m>\vec v + \vec w</m> (see
part (b)).
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
\vec v + \vec w = \vec v - 3\vec v = -2\vec v
= \left\langle -2,\ -4,\ -2\sqrt{11}\right\rangle.
</md>
Therefore
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\vec v + \vec w}{\|\vec v + \vec w\|}
\amp = \frac{-2\vec v}{2\|\vec v\|} = -\frac{\vec v}{\|\vec v\|}
= -\frac14\left\langle 1,\ 2,\ \sqrt{11}\right\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left\langle -\frac14,\ -\frac12,\ -\frac{\sqrt{11}}{4}\right\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
Note that <m>\vec v + \vec w</m> points opposite to <m>\vec v</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 2 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe2-q2">
<statement>
<p>
Consider the two vectors <m>\vec u = \langle 2,-1,3\rangle</m> and
<m>\vec v = \langle 1,2,2\rangle</m>. Write the vector <m>\vec u</m> as
the sum of two vectors, one of which is parallel to <m>\vec v</m> and the
other one is perpendicular to it.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
\vec u\cdot\vec v = 2 - 2 + 6 = 6
\qquad\text{and}\qquad
\|\vec v\|^2 = 1 + 4 + 4 = 9.
</md>
Then
<md>
<mrow>\vec u_{\parallel} = \operatorname{proj}_{\vec v}\vec u
\amp = \left(\frac{\vec u\cdot\vec v}{\|\vec v\|^2}\right)\vec v
= \frac69\langle 1,2,2\rangle
= \left\langle \frac23,\ \frac43,\ \frac43\right\rangle,</mrow>
<mrow>\vec u_{\perp} = \vec u - \vec u_{\parallel}
\amp = \langle 2,-1,3\rangle - \left\langle \frac23,\ \frac43,\ \frac43\right\rangle
= \left\langle \frac43,\ -\frac73,\ \frac53\right\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
Check: <m>\vec u_{\perp}\cdot\vec v = \frac43 - \frac{14}{3} + \frac{10}{3} = 0</m>.
Hence
<md>
\underbrace{\langle 2,-1,3\rangle}_{\vec u}
= \underbrace{\left\langle \frac23,\ \frac43,\ \frac43\right\rangle}_{\vec u_{\parallel}}
+ \underbrace{\left\langle \frac43,\ -\frac73,\ \frac53\right\rangle}_{\vec u_{\perp}}.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 3 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe2-q3">
<introduction>
<p>
Parametrize the following curves.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="spe2-q3-a">
<statement>
<p>
The quarter-ellipse from <m>(0,3)</m> to <m>(-2,0)</m> centered at the
origin.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The ellipse is
<m>\left(\frac{x}{2}\right)^2 + \left(\frac{y}{3}\right)^2 = 1</m>, so
<md>
x(t) = 2\cos t, \qquad y(t) = 3\sin t, \qquad \frac{\pi}{2} \le t \le \pi.
</md>
Check: <m>t = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> gives <m>(0,3)</m> and <m>t = \pi</m>
gives <m>(-2,0)</m>.
</p>
<image width="34%">
<shortdescription>The ellipse with semi-axes 2 and 3 centered at the origin, dashed, with the quarter from (0,3) counterclockwise to (minus 2,0) drawn solid with an arrow.</shortdescription>
<latex-image><![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.75]
  \draw[-stealth] (-3,0) -- (2.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[-stealth] (0,-3.6) -- (0,3.9) node[above] {$y$};
  \draw[dashed, gray] (0,0) ellipse (2 and 3);
  \draw[very thick, blue!70!black, -stealth] (0,3) arc[start angle=90, end angle=180, x radius=2, y radius=3];
  \fill (0,3) circle (2pt) node[above right] {$(0,3)$};
  \fill (-2,0) circle (2pt) node[below left] {$(-2,0)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]></latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe2-q3-b">
<statement>
<p>
The circle of radius 3 centered at <m>(-1,2)</m>, traced once
<em>clockwise</em> starting at <m>(2,2)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>(x+1)^2 + (y-2)^2 = 3^2</m>. A minus sign on the sine term makes the
motion clockwise:
<md>
x(t) = -1 + 3\cos t, \qquad y(t) = 2 - 3\sin t, \qquad 0 \le t \le 2\pi.
</md>
Check: <m>t = 0</m> gives <m>(2,2)</m>; <m>t = \frac{\pi}{2}</m> gives
<m>(-1,-1)</m>, so the point moves down first (clockwise).
</p>
<image width="38%">
<shortdescription>The circle of radius 3 centered at (minus 1,2), with the starting point (2,2) marked and an arrow showing the clockwise direction of motion.</shortdescription>
<latex-image><![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.55]
  \draw[-stealth] (-4.8,0) -- (3.4,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[-stealth] (0,-1.8) -- (0,5.8) node[above] {$y$};
  \draw[very thick, blue!70!black] (-1,2) circle (3);
  \draw[very thick, blue!70!black, -stealth] (2,2) arc[start angle=0, end angle=-40, radius=3];
  \fill (-1,2) circle (2.5pt) node[below] {$(-1,2)$};
  \fill (2,2) circle (2.5pt) node[right] {$(2,2)$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]></latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe2-q3-c">
<statement>
<p>
The line segment from <m>(2,-1)</m> to <m>(-1,3)</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<md>
<mrow>\vec r(t) \amp = \langle 2,-1\rangle + t\,\langle -1-2,\ 3-(-1)\rangle</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \langle 2,-1\rangle + t\,\langle -3,4\rangle,</mrow>
</md>
i.e.
<md>
x(t) = 2 - 3t, \qquad y(t) = -1 + 4t, \qquad 0 \le t \le 1.
</md>
Check: <m>t = 0</m> gives <m>(2,-1)</m> and <m>t = 1</m> gives
<m>(-1,3)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 4 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe2-q4">
<statement>
<p>
Consider an ellipse whose <alert>center</alert> is at the origin. Assume
that it has <alert>foci</alert> <m>(0,-4)</m> and <m>(0,4)</m>, and suppose
that the length of its <alert>minor axis</alert> is 6. Assume that the
major axis is in the <m>y</m>-direction. What is the equation of this
ellipse?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Foci on the <m>y</m>-axis: <m>c = 4</m>. Minor axis <m>2b = 6</m>, so
<m>b = 3</m>. For an ellipse, <m>a^2 = b^2 + c^2 = 9 + 16 = 25</m>, so
<m>a = 5</m>. With the major axis vertical,
<md>
\frac{x^2}{b^2} + \frac{y^2}{a^2} = 1
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\boxed{\frac{x^2}{3^2} + \frac{y^2}{5^2} = 1}
</md>
</p>
<image width="34%">
<shortdescription>The vertical ellipse with semi-axes 3 and 5 centered at the origin and foci F1 at (0,4) and F2 at (0,minus 4); a right triangle joins the origin, F1 and the point (3,0), with legs c equals 4 and b equals 3 and hypotenuse a equals 5.</shortdescription>
<latex-image><![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.42]
  \draw[-stealth] (-4.5,0) -- (4.8,0) node[right] {$x$};
  \draw[-stealth] (0,-6) -- (0,6.4) node[above] {$y$};
  \draw[thick, blue!70!black] (0,0) ellipse (3 and 5);
  \draw[ultra thick, green!50!black] (0,0) -- (0,4) node[midway, left] {$c=4$};
  \draw[ultra thick, magenta] (0,0) -- (3,0) node[midway, below] {$b=3$};
  \draw[thick] (0,4) -- (3,0) node[midway, above right] {$a=5$};
  \fill (0,4) circle (4pt) node[left] {$F_1$};
  \fill (0,-4) circle (4pt) node[left] {$F_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}]]></latex-image>
</image>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 5 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe2-q5">
<statement>
<p>
Find a unit vector that is perpendicular to the <m>xz</m>-plane and points
in the negative <m>y</m>-direction. If the tail of this vector is placed at
<m>(2,-1,3)</m>, where is its tip?
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Perpendicular to the <m>xz</m>-plane means parallel to the <m>y</m>-axis:
<m>\langle 0,c,0\rangle</m>. Length <m>1</m> and pointing in the negative
<m>y</m>-direction gives <m>c = -1</m>:
<md>
-\hat\jmath = \langle 0,-1,0\rangle.
</md>
Tip <m>=</m> tail <m>+</m> vector <m>= (2,\ -1-1,\ 3) = (2,-2,3)</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 6 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe2-q6">
<introduction>
<p>
Mark the following statements as True or False. If a statement is true,
prove it and if a statement is false, provide a counter-example.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="spe2-q6-a">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\vec u</m> and <m>\vec v</m> be any two vectors in
<m>\mathbb R^3</m>. Then
<m>\|\vec u + \vec v\|^2 + \|\vec u - \vec v\|^2 = 2\|\vec u\|^2 + 2\|\vec v\|^2</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<alert>True.</alert> Use <m>\|\vec a\|^2 = \vec a\cdot\vec a</m> and the
distributive and commutative properties of the dot product:
<md>
<mrow>\|\vec u + \vec v\|^2 \amp = (\vec u + \vec v)\cdot(\vec u + \vec v)
= \|\vec u\|^2 + 2\,\vec u\cdot\vec v + \|\vec v\|^2,</mrow>
<mrow>\|\vec u - \vec v\|^2 \amp = (\vec u - \vec v)\cdot(\vec u - \vec v)
= \|\vec u\|^2 - 2\,\vec u\cdot\vec v + \|\vec v\|^2.</mrow>
</md>
Adding, the <m>\pm 2\,\vec u\cdot\vec v</m> terms cancel, leaving
<m>2\|\vec u\|^2 + 2\|\vec v\|^2</m>. (Geometrically: the sum of the
squares of the diagonals of a parallelogram equals the sum of the squares
of its four sides.)
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe2-q6-b">
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\vec u</m>, <m>\vec v</m>, and <m>\vec w</m> be non-zero vectors
such that <m>\vec u\cdot\vec v = \vec u\cdot\vec w</m>. Then
<m>\vec v = \vec w</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<alert>False.</alert> Counter-example:
<m>\vec u = \langle 1,0,0\rangle</m>, <m>\vec v = \langle 1,2,0\rangle</m>,
<m>\vec w = \langle 1,0,5\rangle</m>. Then
<m>\vec u\cdot\vec v = 1 = \vec u\cdot\vec w</m>, but
<m>\vec v \ne \vec w</m>. (In general
<m>\vec u\cdot(\vec v - \vec w) = 0</m> only says
<m>\vec v - \vec w</m> is perpendicular to <m>\vec u</m>, not that it is
<m>\vec 0</m>.)
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

<!-- ===================== Question 7 ===================== -->
<exercise xml:id="spe2-q7">
<introduction>
<p>
Consider the points <m>P(1,0,1)</m>, <m>Q(2,1,0)</m>, and <m>R(0,2,3)</m>.
</p>
</introduction>

<task xml:id="spe2-q7-a">
<statement>
<p>
Find an equation of the plane that contains <m>P</m>, <m>Q</m>, and
<m>R</m>.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
<m>\overrightarrow{PQ} = \langle 1,1,-1\rangle</m> and
<m>\overrightarrow{PR} = \langle -1,2,2\rangle</m>. A normal vector is
<md>
<mrow>\vec n = \overrightarrow{PQ}\times\overrightarrow{PR}
\amp = \begin{vmatrix} \hat\imath \amp \hat\jmath \amp \hat k \\ 1 \amp 1 \amp -1 \\ -1 \amp 2 \amp 2 \end{vmatrix}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = (2+2)\,\hat\imath - (2-1)\,\hat\jmath + (2+1)\,\hat k
= \langle 4,-1,3\rangle.</mrow>
</md>
Using the point <m>P</m>:
<m>4(x-1) - (y-0) + 3(z-1) = 0</m>, i.e.
<md>
\boxed{4x - y + 3z = 7}.
</md>
Check: <m>Q</m>: <m>8 - 1 + 0 = 7</m> <m>\checkmark</m>; <m>R</m>:
<m>0 - 2 + 9 = 7</m> <m>\checkmark</m>.
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe2-q7-b">
<statement>
<p>
Find parametric equations of the line through <m>S(5,2,5)</m> that is
perpendicular to the plane in part (a).
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The line is parallel to the normal <m>\vec n = \langle 4,-1,3\rangle</m>:
<md>
x(t) = 5 + 4t, \qquad y(t) = 2 - t, \qquad z(t) = 5 + 3t, \qquad t \in \mathbb R.
</md>
</p>
</solution>
</task>

<task xml:id="spe2-q7-c">
<statement>
<p>
Find the point where the line in part (b) meets the plane, and use it to
find the distance from <m>S</m> to the plane.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
Substitute into <m>4x - y + 3z = 7</m>:
<md>
4(5+4t) - (2-t) + 3(5+3t) = 7
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad 33 + 26t = 7
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad t = -1.
</md>
The line meets the plane at <m>F = (5-4,\ 2+1,\ 5-3) = (1,3,2)</m> (check:
<m>4 - 3 + 6 = 7</m> <m>\checkmark</m>). The distance from <m>S</m> to the
plane is
<md>
\left\|\overrightarrow{FS}\right\| = \|\langle 4,-1,3\rangle\|
= \sqrt{16 + 1 + 9} = \sqrt{26} \approx 5.10.
</md>
(Same answer from the projection formula:
<m>\dfrac{|\vec n\cdot\overrightarrow{PS}|}{\|\vec n\|}
= \dfrac{|\langle 4,-1,3\rangle\cdot\langle 4,2,4\rangle|}{\sqrt{26}}
= \dfrac{26}{\sqrt{26}} = \sqrt{26}</m>.)
</p>
</solution>
</task>
</exercise>

</worksheet>
